WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.530
A writer may finish uploading a new data file before it publishes the table change.

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:10.445
If a reader treated every physical file as a member, it could read unfinished work.

00:00:10.445 --> 00:00:14.025
Iceberg readers instead follow the committed metadata state.

00:00:15.525 --> 00:00:22.350
In this publication model, the catalog pointer initially names metadata version one, which references file A.

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:24.605
File B can already exist.

00:00:24.605 --> 00:00:29.090
Preparing metadata version two still does not change the reader’s view.

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:35.515
Successful publication changes the pointer so readers can resolve a state referencing both A and B.

00:00:37.015 --> 00:00:40.780
Publication must check the writer’s expected starting state.

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:45.675
If another writer has changed it, a blind replacement could lose that change.

00:00:45.675 --> 00:00:50.040
This lesson models the boundary, not a distributed catalog service.

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:55.490
Run the real stale schema commit lesson to observe requirement validation in the local catalog.
